e-43","page_id":"/docs/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/architecture#7-deployment-architecture","content":"7\\. Deployment Architecture"},"46":{"id":"/docs/architecture-44","page_id":"/docs/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/architecture#71-runtime-environment-requirements","content":"7.1 Runtime Environment Requirements"},"47":{"id":"/docs/architecture-45","page_id":"/docs/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/architecture#72-deployment-topology-structure","content":"7.2 Deployment Topology Structure"},"48":{"id":"/docs/architecture-46","page_id":"/docs/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/architecture#73-scalability-design","content":"7.3 Scalability Design"},"49":{"id":"/docs/architecture-47","page_id":"/docs/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/architecture#74-monitoring-and-operations","content":"7.4 Monitoring and Operations"},"50":{"id":"/docs/architecture-48","page_id":"/docs/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/architecture#appendix-a--component-inventory","content":"Appendix A — Component Inventory"},"51":{"id":"/docs/architecture-49","page_id":"/docs/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/architecture#appendix-b--onboarding-guide-for-new-contributors","content":"Appendix B — Onboarding Guide for New Contributors"},"52":{"id":"/docs/architecture-50","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture","content":"**System:** Unipi — all-in-one extension suite for the Pi coding agent (`@pi-unipi/unipi` v2.18.x)\n&#x2A;*Document type:** C4 architecture model (Context → Container → Component → Code → Deployment)\n&#x2A;*Generated:** 2026-09-16 06:56:42 (UTC)"},"53":{"id":"/docs/architecture-51","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#11-architecture-design-philosophy","content":"Unipi is not a standalone application. It is a **plugin suite** that loads into an existing host — the Pi coding agent, a terminal-based AI coding assistant — and extends it with new tools, slash commands, lifecycle hooks, and terminal-UI overlays. Every architectural decision in the codebase follows from three constraints that this positioning imposes:"},"54":{"id":"/docs/architecture-52","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#11-architecture-design-philosophy","content":"**The host owns the main loop.** No Unipi package runs its own event loop, HTTP listener (with the single exception of the kanboard board server and the trajectory server), or scheduler. Everything is triggered by the host: a tool call from the LLM, a slash command from the developer, or a lifecycle hook such as `session_start`. Packages therefore behave as **reactive registrations** rather than services."},"55":{"id":"/docs/architecture-53","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#11-architecture-design-philosophy","content":"**The agent's context window is the scarcest resource.** Compaction, bounded output truncation of helper results, token budgets for delegated children, and deterministic (non-LLM) summarization are all expressions of a single principle: never let a feature flood the parent conversation. This principle explains why `@pi-unipi/core` ships a `bounded-output` utility and why `get_helper_result` truncates before returning."},"56":{"id":"/docs/architecture-54","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#11-architecture-design-philosophy","content":"**Optional peers must never become hard dependencies.** With \\~24 independently publishable packages, any package may be absent or disabled. Cross-package cooperation is therefore designed around **discovery** (`MODULE_READY` events), **shared synchronous read-models** (`Symbol.for` global slots), and **file-format contracts** (kanboard parses markdown written by workflow/milestone) instead of direct imports. The kernel `@pi-unipi/core` is the only universally shared code."},"57":{"id":"/docs/architecture-55","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#11-architecture-design-philosophy","content":"From these constraints emerge the operational values visible throughout the code: **config-gated, fail-closed registration** (a disabled module registers nothing), **fail-soft backends** (a missing MemPalace or a failed SQLite init degrades rather than crashes), **crash-safe persistence** (temp-write/fsync/rename with versioned manifests), and **centralised process-tree lifecycle** (timeouts, ESC abort propagation, and Windows `taskkill` tree termination are handled in hubs)."},"58":{"id":"/docs/architecture-56","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#12-core-architecture-patterns","content":"Pattern"},"59":{"id":"/docs/architecture-57","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#12-core-architecture-patterns","content":"Where it appears"},"60":{"id":"/docs/architecture-58","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#12-core-architecture-patterns","content":"Purpose"},"61":{"id":"/docs/architecture-59","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#12-core-architecture-patterns","content":"**Plugin suite on a thin shared kernel**"},"62":{"id":"/docs/architecture-60","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#12-core-architecture-patterns","content":"`@pi-unipi/core` is the only internal dependency of nearly every package"},"63":{"id":"/docs/architecture-61","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#12-core-architecture-patterns","content":"Minimise blast radius; keep packages independently loadable and publishable"},"64":{"id":"/docs/architecture-62","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/arc